# Algorithms, Algorithms, Algorithms

**Authors:** J. Michael, J. Voas

## Abstract
Everyone depends on the use of algorithms for just about every aspect of their daily lives. But how many noncomputer scientists know that? It is reasonable to assume that it is a small percentage of the population. Further, many people may be unfamiliar with the term algorithm. (And possibly, even technical experts have not considered the huge role that algorithms play in their lives and professions.) Algorithms are like recipes. An algorithm specifies a process or set of rules for accomplishing one or more tasks. For example, the Advanced Encryption Standard algorithm contains steps for encrypting and decrypting information. Algorithms are at the heart of computation and highlighted in the title of Niklaus Wirth’s book, Algorithms + Data Structures = Programs. Modern automobiles contain embedded systems, each of which performs computations. There is the seemingly endless number of devices comprising the Internet of Things—modern appliances in our kitchens, medical equipment in health-care facilities, and robotic equipment in factories, to name a few—that automate routine tasks with machine-to-machine interaction. We also rely on algorithms for mission-essential systems, such as the routing of traffic through the Internet; tracking and identifying objects as part of a missile defense system; managing fleets of commercial aircraft; detecting anomalies in financial markets, such as insider trading; and forecasting the weather. Digital Object Identifier 10.1109/MC.2020.3016534 Date of current version: 21 October 2020 Algorithms, Algorithms, Algorithms

